# Arara Grammar

Status: **RATIFIED — D001 contract, `D001-13`.** Frozen for the D002–D004
implementation of the initial subset. Any change requires a new decision that
names the superseded rule.
Scope: the concrete syntax of the initial subset, expressed as an EBNF grammar over
the token stream produced by `lexical.md`. Normative for `ARA-PARSE-*`
diagnostics.
Related: `lexical.md` (tokens, statement separation), `semantics.md` (typing,
JVM mapping), `conformance-plan.md` (syntax test matrix).

## 1. Conventions

| Notation | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `Name ::= ... .` | production; the period terminates it |
| `a b` | concatenation |
| `a \| b` | ordered alternative (first token decides; see §6) |
| `{ a }` | zero or more repetitions |
| `[ a ]` | optional |
| `"text"` | exact token text (keyword or punctuation) |
| `UPPER` | a token class from `lexical.md` §3 |
| `(* ... *)` | explanatory comment, not grammar |

Terminals used here and defined in `lexical.md`:
`IDENT`, `INT_LITERAL`, `STRING_LITERAL`, `SEMI`, `EOF`.
Keywords and punctuation are written as quoted text because their token class and
their spelling are identical.

`SEMI` is produced by the statement-separation rules in `lexical.md` §10. The
grammar never sees `NEWLINE` or `RESERVED`; a `RESERVED` token has already
produced a lexical error, and parsing of that unit stops.

## 2. Grammar

```ebnf
Program        ::= [ FunctionList ] EOF .

FunctionList   ::= FunctionDecl FunctionTail .

FunctionTail   ::= Separator [ FunctionList ]
                 | (* empty *) .

FunctionDecl   ::= "fun" IDENT "(" [ ParameterList ] ")" [ ":" Type ] Block .

ParameterList  ::= Parameter { "," Parameter } .
Parameter      ::= IDENT ":" Type .

Type           ::= "Int" | "Boolean" | "String" | "Unit" .

Block          ::= "{" [ StatementList ] "}" .

StatementList  ::= Statement StatementTail .

StatementTail  ::= Separator [ StatementList ]
                 | (* empty *) .

Separator      ::= SEMI .

Statement      ::= LocalDecl
                 | IdentStatement
                 | ReturnStatement
                 | IfStatement
                 | Block .

LocalDecl      ::= ( "val" | "var" ) IDENT ":" Type "=" Expression .

IdentStatement ::= IDENT ( "=" Expression | CallSuffix ) .

CallSuffix     ::= "(" [ ArgumentList ] ")" .
ArgumentList   ::= Expression { "," Expression } .

ReturnStatement ::= "return" [ Expression ] .

IfStatement    ::= "if" "(" Expression ")" Block
                   [ "else" ( Block | IfStatement ) ] .

Expression     ::= OrExpression .

OrExpression   ::= AndExpression { "||" AndExpression } .

AndExpression  ::= EqualityExpression { "&&" EqualityExpression } .

EqualityExpression ::= RelationalExpression
                       { ( "==" | "!=" ) RelationalExpression } .

RelationalExpression ::= AdditiveExpression
                         { ( "<" | "<=" | ">" | ">=" ) AdditiveExpression } .

AdditiveExpression ::= MultiplicativeExpression
                       { ( "+" | "-" ) MultiplicativeExpression } .

MultiplicativeExpression ::= UnaryExpression
                             { ( "*" | "/" | "%" ) UnaryExpression } .

UnaryExpression ::= ( "-" | "!" ) UnaryExpression
                  | PrimaryExpression .

PrimaryExpression ::= INT_LITERAL
                    | STRING_LITERAL
                    | "true"
                    | "false"
                    | IDENT [ CallSuffix ]
                    | "(" Expression ")" .
```

`FunctionTail` and `StatementTail` are right-recursive tails. They exist for two
reasons: they accept an optional separator after the last function or statement
without creating an ambiguity, and they keep the grammar LL(1) (§6).

## 3. What the grammar deliberately omits

1. There is no expression form for assignment; assignment exists only as
   `IdentStatement`.
2. There are no expression-bodied functions. A function body is always a `Block`.
3. There are no single-statement `if`/`else` bodies. Braces are mandatory.
4. There are no loops, classes, lambdas, `when`, type aliases, packages, imports,
   annotations, or exception syntax. Their keywords are reserved in
   `lexical.md` §6 and are rejected before parsing.
5. There are no declarations inside blocks other than local bindings. Nested
   functions are not part of the subset.
6. There is no empty statement and no empty declaration. `Separator` is a
   separator, never a statement. Consequently: a leading `SEMI` in a block or at
   the start of a file is `ARA-PARSE-0100`; two consecutive `SEMI` tokens are
   `ARA-PARSE-0100` at the second; a single trailing `SEMI` after the last
   statement or declaration is accepted; and a block or file containing only
   `SEMI` tokens is `ARA-PARSE-0100` (a declaration or statement was required
   before the separator). This matches `lexical.md` §10 rules 57 and 58.
6a. A `SEMI` is **required** between two statements in the same block and
   between two top-level declarations. On a single line, `fun a() {} fun b() {}`
   is `ARA-PARSE-0100` (expected `;`), and `val x: Int = 1 val y: Int = 2` is
   `ARA-PARSE-0101` because the preceding token (`1`) completed an expression
   (§7 code selection). Both codes are deliberate: the position of the missing
   separator determines which expectation was violated. This makes statement
   boundaries explicit and prevents an expression from silently ending where a
   new statement was intended.
7. There is no chained assignment, no compound assignment, and no increment or
   decrement.
8. `Unit` is syntactically accepted anywhere a `Type` is accepted. Its use as
   anything other than a function return type is rejected semantically
   (`semantics.md` §2.4, §3.3, §3.6).

## 4. Statement termination

The grammar is written against the post-separation token stream. Two rules from
`lexical.md` §10 are visible here as parse errors rather than grammar
productions:

- `ARA-PARSE-0107` — a `)` that ends a parameter list or an `if` condition is
  followed by `SEMI`, so the required `Block` (or the `:` of a return type) is not
  on the same line.
- `ARA-PARSE-0108` — an `else` appears after a `SEMI` that follows the closing `}`
  of the then-block.

Both are detectable with one token of lookahead plus the previous token, and both
must be reported with these codes rather than the generic `ARA-PARSE-0100`.

A third rule is visible here as a parse error: a `SEMI` immediately after `{`, at
the start of a file, or twice in a row is `ARA-PARSE-0100`, because `Separator`
has no empty alternative and `Program`/`Block` require content before the first
separator (`syntax.ebnf` §3 items 6 and 6a).

## 5. Derivation of the canonical example

Source:

```arara
fun main() {
    val answer: Int = 40 + 2
    println(answer)
}
```

Token stream after separation (the line feed after `)` and after the final `}`
are not shown because no `SEMI` is produced there; the line feeds after `2` and
after the inner `)` produce `SEMI`):

```text
fun IDENT(main) ( ) { val IDENT(answer) : Int = INT_LITERAL(40) + INT_LITERAL(2)
SEMI IDENT(println) ( IDENT(answer) ) SEMI }
```

Derivation:

```text
Program
  FunctionList
    FunctionDecl
      "fun" IDENT(main) "(" [ParameterList] ")" [":" Type] Block
      Block
        "{"
        StatementList
          Statement
            LocalDecl
              "val" IDENT(answer) ":" Type("Int") "=" Expression
              Expression -> OrExpression -> ... -> AdditiveExpression
                MultiplicativeExpression(INT_LITERAL(40))
                "+"
                MultiplicativeExpression(INT_LITERAL(2))
          StatementTail
            Separator
              SEMI
            StatementList
              Statement
                IdentStatement
                  IDENT(println) CallSuffix
                    "(" ArgumentList ")"
                      Expression -> ... -> PrimaryExpression(IDENT(answer))
              StatementTail
                Separator
                  SEMI
        "}"
    FunctionTail
      (* empty: end of file *)
```

The example is therefore derivable, and it exercises: a declaration, a binary
expression, a call statement, and newline-driven separation.

## 6. Parser classification

The grammar as written is **LL(1)**. This is a claim about this grammar text
only; it is not a claim that every possible formulation of Arara's syntax is
LL(1), and no broader grammar class is claimed.

The analysis was performed mechanically with a FIRST/FOLLOW checker over the
grammar block above; the checker is an ad-hoc audit script and is deliberately
**not** part of this repository, because D001 permits documentation and
configuration only. Its output is reproduced in §6.3. A human summary follows.

### 6.1 FIRST and FOLLOW

Nullable nonterminals: `FunctionTail` and `StatementTail` only.

The seven `Expression` precedence levels (`OrExpression` through
`MultiplicativeExpression`, plus `UnaryExpression`) are omitted from this table
because each has the same FIRST set as `Expression` and differs only in FOLLOW at
the operator token above it. They are listed individually in the checker output
and are covered by conformance-plan §4's per-level obligations.

| Nonterminal | FIRST | FOLLOW |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Program` | `fun`, `EOF` | `$` |
| `FunctionList` | `fun` | `EOF` |
| `FunctionTail` | `SEMI` | `EOF` |
| `FunctionDecl` | `fun` | `SEMI`, `EOF` |
| `ParameterList` | `IDENT` | `)` |
| `Parameter` | `IDENT` | `,`, `)` |
| `Type` | `Int`, `Boolean`, `String`, `Unit` | `)`, `,`, `=`, `{` |
| `Block` | `{` | `SEMI`, `}`, `else`, `EOF` |
| `StatementList` | `IDENT`, `if`, `return`, `val`, `var`, `{` | `}` |
| `StatementTail` | `SEMI` | `}` |
| `Separator` | `SEMI` | `IDENT`, `fun`, `if`, `return`, `val`, `var`, `{`, `}`, `EOF` |
| `Statement` | `IDENT`, `if`, `return`, `val`, `var`, `{` | `SEMI`, `}` |
| `LocalDecl` | `val`, `var` | `SEMI`, `}` |
| `IdentStatement` | `IDENT` | `SEMI`, `}` |
| `CallSuffix` | `(` | binary operators, `%`, `,`, `)`, `SEMI`, `}` |
| `ArgumentList` | expression-start | `)` |
| `ReturnStatement` | `return` | `SEMI`, `}` |
| `IfStatement` | `if` | `SEMI`, `}` |
| `Expression` | expression-start | `)`, `,`, `SEMI`, `}` |
| `PrimaryExpression` | `(`, `IDENT`, `INT_LITERAL`, `STRING_LITERAL`, `true`, `false` | binary operators, `%`, `,`, `)`, `SEMI`, `}` |

`expression-start` is `{ "!", "(", "-", IDENT, INT_LITERAL, STRING_LITERAL, "false", "true" }`.

### 6.2 Why each choice is decidable with one token

| Choice point | Decision | Conflict |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `[ FunctionList ]` in `Program` | `fun` vs `EOF` | disjoint |
| `FunctionTail`: separator or end | `SEMI` vs `EOF` | disjoint |
| `[ StatementList ]` in `Block` | statement-start vs `}` | disjoint |
| `StatementTail`: separator or end | `SEMI` vs `}` | disjoint |
| `Statement` alternatives | `val`/`var`, `IDENT`, `return`, `if`, `{` | pairwise disjoint |
| `PrimaryExpression` alternatives | `(`, `IDENT`, literals, `true`, `false` | pairwise disjoint |
| `IDENT [ CallSuffix ]` | `(` vs FOLLOW(`PrimaryExpression`) | `(` is not in FOLLOW, so disjoint |
| `[ ParameterList ]` | `IDENT` vs `)` | disjoint |
| `[ ArgumentList ]` | expression-start vs `)` | disjoint |
| `[ ":" Type ]` | `:` vs `{` | disjoint |
| `[ "else" ... ]` | `else` vs FOLLOW(`IfStatement`) | `else` is not in FOLLOW |
| `( "-" \| "!" )` in `UnaryExpression` | `-`/`!` vs FIRST(`PrimaryExpression`) | disjoint; `PrimaryExpression` starts with `(`, `IDENT`, a literal, `true`, or `false` |
| `Block \| IfStatement` after `else` | `{` vs `if` | disjoint |

`IdentStatement` is left-factored: after `IDENT` the next token is `=` or `(`,
which is disjoint. This is the only factoring the grammar requires.

There is no dangling-`else` ambiguity because every branch body is a `Block`:
an `else` can only follow the `}` that closes the nearest preceding `if`, and no
unbraced `if` can appear as a body. `else` is therefore in FOLLOW(`Block`) only
in the position the production expects. There is no unary/binary `-` ambiguity
because unary `-` is reachable only where an expression is expected and binary
`-` only after a complete operand; `1 - 2` and `1 -2` are the same token sequence
and both parse as subtraction.

### 6.3 Mechanical check output

```text
productions: 27
nullable nonterminals: ['FunctionTail', 'StatementTail']
LL(1) result: no conflicts
```

Re-running this check is a D002 obligation: the D002 build must contain an
equivalent automated first/follow test, and §6.2 must remain true after any
grammar change.

### 6.4 Why tail productions rather than `{ Separators ... }`

An earlier formulation used `[ Separators ]` before, between, and after items.
That formulation is ambiguous for a run of two or more `SEMI` tokens: the parser
cannot tell whether a separator belongs to the preceding repetition or the
following optional prefix without unbounded lookahead. The tail form
(`Separator [ ... ]`) makes each separator have exactly one owner and is LL(1).
This is recorded because the difference is not cosmetic: it changes the accepted
language for repeated separators.

## 7. Parse diagnostics

All are `ERROR` except `ARA-PARSE-0199`, which is a `NOTE`. The primary span is
the offending token, or the empty span at end of file when the token is missing.

| Code | Condition | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ARA-PARSE-0100` | Unexpected token for the expected production | Related note lists the expected token set |
| `ARA-PARSE-0101` | Unexpected token immediately after a complete expression or literal | Covers `123abc` and `f() g()`; takes precedence over `ARA-PARSE-0100` when the preceding token completed an expression |
| `ARA-PARSE-0102` | Expected `)` | Reported at the token that is not `)` |
| `ARA-PARSE-0103` | Expected `}` | Reported at the token that is not `}` |
| `ARA-PARSE-0104` | Expected a type name after `:` | Reported at the token that is not a type |
| `ARA-PARSE-0105` | Expected an expression | Reported at the token that cannot start an expression |
| `ARA-PARSE-0106` | Expected a function name after `fun` | Reported at the token that is not `IDENT` |
| `ARA-PARSE-0107` | A block or return type after `)` is not on the same line as `)` | Rule from `lexical.md` §10 |
| `ARA-PARSE-0108` | `else` is not on the same line as the preceding `}` | Rule from `lexical.md` §10 |
| `ARA-PARSE-0109` | A statement begins with an expression that is neither a call nor an assignment | e.g. `1 + 2` or `-x`; also used when `if` appears where an expression is required, because `if` is not an expression |
| `ARA-PARSE-0110` | Expected `=` or `(` after an identifier in statement position | Prevents a bare identifier statement |
| `ARA-PARSE-0111` | Expected `(` after a function name in a declaration | Reported at the token that is not `(` |
| `ARA-PARSE-0112` | Expected `:` after a parameter name | |
| `ARA-PARSE-0113` | Expected `,` or `)` in a parameter or argument list | |
| `ARA-PARSE-0114` | Expected a function body block after a function header | Reported at `EOF` or the offending token |
| `ARA-PARSE-0115` | Expected `{` | After `if (expr)`, after `else`, or where a body was required |
| `ARA-PARSE-0116` | Expected `(` after `if` | |
| `ARA-PARSE-0117` | Unmatched `)` | A `)` with no open parenthesis; the separation counter never goes below zero (`lexical.md` §10 rule 52) |
| `ARA-PARSE-0199` | Parse error limit reached (100 errors) | `NOTE`-severity companion diagnostic; parsing stops |

A conforming parser MUST report exactly one of these codes for a single
syntactic defect. It MUST NOT report both `ARA-PARSE-0100` and a more specific code for the
same token.

Code selection is deterministic:

1. If the failure is `ARA-PARSE-0107` or `ARA-PARSE-0108` (newline-driven
   separation), report that code, never `ARA-PARSE-0100`.
2. Otherwise, if the token that fails to parse immediately follows a token that
   completed a `PrimaryExpression` or a full `Expression`, report `ARA-PARSE-0101`.
3. Otherwise report the specific expected-token code (`ARA-PARSE-0102` through
   `ARA-PARSE-0106`, or `ARA-PARSE-0110` through `ARA-PARSE-0117`) when exactly
   one such expectation applies.
4. Otherwise report `ARA-PARSE-0100` with the expected token set as a related
   note.

## 8. Error recovery

Recovery is specified so that negative tests are deterministic:

1. **Top level.** On an error while parsing a `FunctionDecl`, skip tokens until
   `"fun"` at parenthesis depth 0 or `EOF`, then resume `FunctionList`.
2. **Block.** On an error while parsing a `Statement`, skip tokens until `SEMI`,
   `"}"`, or `EOF`, then resume `StatementList` (consuming the separator).
3. **Expression.** On an error inside an expression, do not skip; report once and
   return an error expression to the caller. The enclosing statement then
   synchronizes per rule 2.
4. **Missing closer.** If `}` is missing at `EOF`, report `ARA-PARSE-0103` with a
   note pointing at the opening `{`.
5. **Cascades.** Recovery must not produce a second error for a token already
   reported. The parser tracks the last reported token offset and suppresses
   duplicates at that offset.
6. **Limit.** After 100 `ERROR` diagnostics, stop parsing and emit
   `ARA-PARSE-0199` as a `NOTE`. The resulting AST is discarded.

Recovery never invents a successful parse. A unit with any parse error produces no
AST for later stages.

## 9. Recommended parser strategy

A hand-written recursive-descent parser with:

- one token of lookahead, plus the previous token to produce `ARA-PARSE-0107` and `ARA-PARSE-0108`;
- one function per precedence level, or precedence climbing within the binary
  levels (either strategy accepts exactly this grammar; the choice is an
  implementation detail that must not change the accepted language);
- panic-mode recovery as specified in §8;
- no parser generator.

This is recommended, not mandated: any implementation whose accepted language is
exactly this grammar and whose diagnostics match §7 conforms.

## 10. Non-goals

This grammar does not define: type inference syntax, generics, string templates,
lambda syntax, expression-bodied functions, single-statement bodies, loop forms,
package/import declarations, annotations, or operator declarations. Constructs
outside the grammar must be rejected; no construct may be accepted with
unspecified behavior.
